      sources: "Sources: Chapter 05, “Dangers”; Learn, custom extension security; Configure a Logic App; Compare Azure Government and global Azure.",
      say: `Each of these dangers ends the same way: a leaver whose access isn't really gone, or a record that says it is. The quietest are the callback problems: no callback on failure, a callback that claims success, or one sent to the wrong cloud. Others are about trust: a forged payload, a broad permission, or a bearer-token extension left behind. And some are about order: doing the work twice, or running the custom task before the account is blocked. Notice the pattern again: each guard is a lint rule or a check that runs every time.`,
      detail: `A failure that sends no callback: the leaver's revocation fails, and nobody knows until the wait ends; guard: Resume runs after failure and time-out; LX-07. A callback that reports success for failed work: a credential stays valid and the record says revoked; guard: status computed from the call; LX-08. The wrong cloud: a GCC High or DoD callback goes to the worldwide Graph and never arrives; guard: graphHost limited to three roots, set from the registry; LX-03; tests for every cloud. A forged or malformed payload: the managed identity's token is sent to a path you didn't intend; guard: PoP authorization with SAS off; Check_request before any call; LX-06. A repeat delivery or a rerun: the downstream system revokes, issues or deletes twice; guard: Idempotency-Key from the task processing result; LX-09. Retries longer than the wait: the callback arrives after the task has already failed; guard: bounded retries; LX-11 against the registry's timeout. Personal data in run history: names, UPNs and emails readable by anyone with run access; guard: secured inputs and outputs; object ID only downstream; LX-10, LX-12. A broad permission on the identity: a compromised Logic App can change any workflow; guard: resume through authorizedApps, no LifecycleWorkflows.ReadWrite.All; CX-04. A custom task before the block: a leaver keeps signing in while revocations run; guard: WX-03 and WX-04 against Book 03's order. A bearer-token extension left in place: weaker than PoP, and it can't be converted; guard: CX-01; recreate it as PoP. The placeholder appid: every call is refused; guard: the builder refuses it; Lab 2 checks the real one. Azure Government policy values guessed: the Logic App rejects Lifecycle Workflows, or accepts the wrong caller; guard: the builder refuses; research gap 12.`,
